---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>3.6 (Day 2) — Combat Engine RPG Build: Tactical Round &amp; Stances</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topics 2.1, 2.3, 2.4, 2.5 · 90-Minute RPG Build (Day 2 of 2).</strong> Yesterday in
    <code>3.6a</code> you built <code>evaluateStrike</code>, <code>playerAttack</code>, and
    <code>monsterAttack</code> inside <code>Combat.java</code>. Today you complete the remaining
    three methods in that same <code>Combat.java</code> file: armor/defend damage mitigation,
    full-round counterattack gating, and multi-stance tactical exchanges.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Continue in Your Existing Files:</strong> Keep working in your
    <code>Combat.java</code> from <code>3.6a</code> alongside
    <a href="../data/CombatCheck.java" download>CombatCheck.java</a> and
    <a href="../data/Dice.java" download>Dice.java</a>. When you finish today's build, all
    <strong>32 checks</strong> across Parts 1–5 will pass!
    <pre><code>javac Dice.java Combat.java CombatCheck.java
java CombatCheck</code></pre>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 3 · Discovery &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: <code>applyMitigatedDamage(int currentHP, int rawDamage, boolean isDefending, int flatShield)</code></h3>

    <p>
        When a character takes damage in an RPG, raw damage is reduced by two things in order:
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Defending Stance:</strong> If <code>isDefending</code> is <code>true</code>,
            incoming <code>rawDamage</code> is cut in half using integer division
            (<code>rawDamage / 2</code>) <em>before</em> the shield is applied.</li>
        <li><strong>Flat Shield / Armor Absorption:</strong> Subtract <code>flatShield</code> from
            the damage.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Discovery Trap 1: The Armor-Heal Bug &amp; Overkill Negatives</h3>
        <p>
            Almost every programmer writes their first draft of damage mitigation like this:
        </p>
        <pre><code>// Naive draft — test this in applyMitigatedDamage and run CombatCheck!
public static int applyMitigatedDamage(int currentHP, int rawDamage, boolean isDefending, int flatShield) {
    if (isDefending) {
        rawDamage = rawDamage / 2;
    }
    int netDamage = rawDamage - flatShield;
    return currentHP - netDamage;
}</code></pre>
        <ol>
            <li>Suppose a hero has <code>currentHP = 20</code> and a tough <code>flatShield = 5</code>.
                A weak Goblin hits them for <code>rawDamage = 3</code> (not defending). Trace the
                naive code above: what is <code>netDamage</code>, and what does
                <code>currentHP - netDamage</code> return? Why did getting hit by a Goblin
                <strong>HEAL</strong> the hero to <code>22 HP</code>?!</li>
            <li>Now suppose <code>currentHP = 12</code> and a Dragon hits for
                <code>rawDamage = 35</code> with <code>flatShield = 2</code>. What happens if you
                don't clamp the final HP at <code>0</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <h4>AP Specification — <code>applyMitigatedDamage</code></h4>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>public static int applyMitigatedDamage(int currentHP, int rawDamage, boolean isDefending, int flatShield)</code></li>
        <li><strong>Rules:</strong>
            <ol>
                <li>If <code>rawDamage &lt;= 0</code>, no damage is taken; return <code>currentHP</code> unchanged.</li>
                <li>If <code>isDefending</code> is <code>true</code>, halve <code>rawDamage</code> via
                    integer division (<code>rawDamage / 2</code>).</li>
                <li>Subtract <code>flatShield</code> to get net damage. If net damage is less than
                    <code>0</code>, clamp net damage to <code>0</code> (a shield never heals!).</li>
                <li>Subtract net damage from <code>currentHP</code>. If the resulting HP is less than
                    <code>0</code>, return <code>0</code>; otherwise return the resulting HP.</li>
            </ol>
        </li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 4 · Discovery &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 4: <code>combatRound(...)</code> — The Zombie Counterattack Trap</h3>

    <p>
        In a turn-based combat exchange, the player attacks first. Subtract the player's damage from
        <code>monsterHP</code>. Then — <strong>and only if the monster is still alive
        (<code>monsterHP &gt; 0</code>)</strong> — the monster counterattacks!
    </p>

    <h4>AP Specification — <code>combatRound</code></h4>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Signature:</strong>
            <pre><code>public static int combatRound(int playerAtk, int monsterAC, int playerDmg,
                              int monsterAtk, int playerAC, int monsterDmg,
                              String monsterName, int monsterHP)</code></pre>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Behavior:</strong>
            <ol>
                <li>Call <code>playerAttack(playerAtk, monsterAC, playerDmg, monsterName)</code> and
                    subtract the returned damage from <code>monsterHP</code>.</li>
                <li>If <code>monsterHP &gt; 0</code>, call
                    <code>monsterAttack(monsterAtk, playerAC, monsterDmg, monsterName)</code>;
                    otherwise print a victory message and skip the monster's turn.</li>
                <li>Return the updated <code>monsterHP</code>.</li>
            </ol>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint — Pass-By-Value Discovery</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>In <code>combatRound</code>, you subtract damage from the parameter
                <code>monsterHP</code>. Why does <code>combatRound</code> ALSO have to
                <code>return monsterHP</code>? If the caller in <code>main</code> wrote
                <code>Combat.combatRound(..., goblinHP);</code> without assigning
                <code>goblinHP = Combat.combatRound(..., goblinHP);</code>, what would
                <code>goblinHP</code> in <code>main</code> still equal after the call, and why?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 5 · AP FRQ Synthesis Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~35 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 5: <code>resolveTacticalExchange(String stance, int playerHP, int monsterHP, int playerDmgDealt, int monsterRawDmg, int flatShield)</code></h3>

    <p>
        Finally, combine String comparison (from <code>3.4</code>), boolean gating (from
        <code>3.5</code>), and your <code>applyMitigatedDamage</code> helper (from Part 3) to
        resolve a complete tactical turn and return the <strong>player's remaining HP</strong>:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th><code>stance</code></th><th>Player's Attack on <code>monsterHP</code></th><th>If Monster Survives (<code>monsterHP &gt; 0</code>), Player Takes:</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>null</code> or <code>"flee"</code></td>
            <td>Player does not attack (escapes or no-op)</td>
            <td>Monster does not attack; return <code>playerHP</code> unchanged</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>"attack"</code></td>
            <td>Subtract <code>playerDmgDealt</code> from <code>monsterHP</code></td>
            <td><code>applyMitigatedDamage(playerHP, monsterRawDmg, false, flatShield)</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>"defend"</code></td>
            <td>Cautious strike: subtract <code>playerDmgDealt / 2</code> from <code>monsterHP</code></td>
            <td><code>applyMitigatedDamage(playerHP, monsterRawDmg, true, flatShield)</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>"charge"</code></td>
            <td>Reckless strike: subtract <code>playerDmgDealt * 2</code> from <code>monsterHP</code></td>
            <td>Shield is dropped! <code>applyMitigatedDamage(playerHP, monsterRawDmg * 2, false, 0)</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Any other string</td>
            <td>Fumbled command: <code>0</code> damage dealt to <code>monsterHP</code></td>
            <td><code>applyMitigatedDamage(playerHP, monsterRawDmg, false, flatShield)</code></td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint — Trace the Stance Trade-Off</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Suppose <code>playerHP = 25</code>, <code>monsterHP = 12</code>,
                <code>playerDmgDealt = 6</code>, <code>monsterRawDmg = 10</code>, and
                <code>flatShield = 5</code>. Trace the player's final HP under all three combat
                stances (<code>"attack"</code>, <code>"defend"</code>, and <code>"charge"</code>).
                Which stance leaves the player with the most HP in this exact scenario, and why?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Day 2 Milestone — Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><code>java CombatCheck</code> reports <strong><code>32 passed, 0 failed</code></strong> across all 5 parts</li>
            <li>You have completed the reflection boxes above and downloaded your <code>.json</code> for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/combat-system.html">Student RPG Guide: Combat System</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/comparing-objects.html">Comparing Objects</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/boolean-logic.html">Boolean Logic</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
